INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(3704,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1740935','The threat to scientific integrity in environmental and occupational medicine.','Tong S, Olsen J.','2005','eminent legitimise irresponsibly subverted whistleblowers sued veracity husten ashford giannasi fernanda tushar ladou lemen icoh bartrip jhmas cory slechta mushak needleman silbergeld ferrence tob daube tob egilman tweedale geoffrey jock kovarik bohme susanna bartrip ernhart scarr geneson whistleblower needleman s15327019eb0301_2 chasing wynder friedberg saffran stinson garne megan fiona 4lancet kuehn ladou lewison mccrary jakovljevic wray nejm200011303432207 michaels wendy needleman scarr ernhart glantz subverting parascandola boreham darby deo silcocks whitley poo 428204a resnik shamoo adil preface lore silbergeld needleman 428206a yach bialous junking','Abstract Over the last century, environmental and occupational medicine has played a significant role in the protection and improvement of public health. However, scientific integrity in this field has been increasingly threatened by pressure from some industries and governments. For example, it has been reported that the tobacco industry manipulated eminent scientists to legitimise their industrial positions, irresponsibly distorted risk and deliberately subverted scientific processes, and influenced many organisations in receipt of tobacco funding. Many environmental whistleblowers were sued and encountered numerous personal attacks. In some countries, scientific findings have been suppressed and distorted, and scientific advisory committees manipulated for political purposes by government agencies. How to respond to these threats is an important challenge for environmental and occupational medicine professionals and their societies. The authors recommend that professional organisations adopt a code of ethics that requires openness from public health professionals; that they not undertake research or use data where they do not have freedom to publish their results if these data have public health implications; that they disclose all possible conflicts; that the veracity of their research results should not be compromised; and that their research independence be protected through professional and legal support. The authors furthermore recommend that research funding for public health not be directly from the industry to the researcher. An independent, intermediate funding scheme should be established to ensure that there is no pressure to analyse data and publish results in bad faith. Such a funding system should also provide equal competition for funds and selection of the best proposals according to standard scientific criteria. Full Text The Full Text of this article is available as a PDF (69.4 KB). Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Abdullah A. S. M., Husten C. G. Promotion of smoking cessation in developing countries: a framework for urgent public health interventions. Thorax. 2004 Jul;59(7):623–630. doi: 10.1136/thx.2003.018820. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Ashford Nicholas A., Castleman Barry, Frank Arthur L., Giannasi Fernanda, Goldman Lynn R., Greenberg Morris, Huff James, Joshi Kant Tushar, LaDou Joseph, Lemen Richard A. The International Commission on Occupational Health (ICOH) and its influence on international organizations. Int J Occup Environ Health. 2002 Apr-Jun;8(2):156–162. doi: 10.1179/107735202800338984. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Bartrip P. W. J. Irving John Selikoff and the strange case of the missing medical degrees. J Hist Med Allied Sci. 2003 Jan;58(1):3–33. doi: 10.1093/jhmas/58.1.3. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Chisholm J., Goldstein G., Cory-Slechta D., Weiss B., Landrigan P., Mushak P., Needleman H. L., Rice D., Rosen J., Silbergeld E. Lead debate goes on. Pediatrics. 1994 Sep;94(3):408–410. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Cohen J. E., Ashley M. J., Ferrence R., Brewster J. M., Goldstein A. O. Institutional addiction to tobacco. Tob Control. 1999 Spring;8(1):70–74. doi: 10.1136/tc.8.1.70. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Daube M. Political history of smoking and health. Tob Control. 2000 Sep;9(3):353–354. doi: 10.1136/tc.9.3.353. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Egilman David, Tweedale Geoffrey, McCulloch Jock, Kovarik William, Castleman Barry, Longo William, Levin Stephen, Bohme Susanna Rankin. P.W.J. Bartrip''s attack on Irving J. Selikoff. Am J Ind Med. 2004 Aug;46(2):151–155. doi: 10.1002/ajim.20047. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Ernhart Claire B., Scarr Sandra, Geneson David F. On being a whistleblower: the Needleman case. Ethics Behav. 1993;3(1):73–93. doi: 10.1207/s15327019eb0301_2. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Fields N., Chapman S. Chasing Ernst L Wynder: 40 years of Philip Morris'' efforts to influence a leading scientist. J Epidemiol Community Health. 2003 Aug;57(8):571–578. doi: 10.1136/jech.57.8.571. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Friedberg M., Saffran B., Stinson T. J., Nelson W., Bennett C. L. Evaluation of conflict of interest in economic analyses of new drugs used in oncology. JAMA. 1999 Oct 20;282(15):1453–1457. doi: 10.1001/jama.282.15.1453. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Garne David, Watson Megan, Chapman Simon, Byrne Fiona. Environmental tobacco smoke research published in the journal Indoor and Built Environment and associations with the tobacco industry. 2005 Feb 26-Mar 4Lancet. 365(9461):804–809. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(05)17990-2. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Kuehn Robert R. Suppression of environmental science. Am J Law Med. 2004;30(2-3):333–369. doi: 10.1177/009885880403000210. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] LaDou Joseph. The asbestos cancer epidemic. Environ Health Perspect. 2004 Mar;112(3):285–290. doi: 10.1289/ehp.6704. [ DOI ] [ PMC free artic